Now let's say the Email Consumer successfully sends the email, but before it commits the Kafka offset, the consumer crashes.

When it restarts, Kafka delivers the same notification again.

The user receives the same email twice.

How would you prevent duplicate notifications?




We need idempotency.

But I want you to explain the implementation.

Suppose we have:

notificationId = N123
channel = EMAIL

The Email Consumer receives it.

First attempt:

N123 + EMAIL
    ↓
Check Idempotency Store → Not found
    ↓
Send Email ✅
    ↓
Consumer crashes ❌

The Kafka offset wasn't committed, so the message is delivered again.

Now:

N123 + EMAIL
    ↓
Check Idempotency Store
    ↓
Already processed?
    ↓
YES → Don't send email again



This Idempotency store can be redis in case of Notiifcation System with short ttl.

But what if consumer fails sent mail but writing to redis failed.so now we dont have idempotency in redis..we wil still prcoes again.



HAve idempotency at provider Level


"We'll use an idempotency key derived from notificationId and channel, such as notificationId + channel. 
The consumer sends this idempotency key to the provider. 
If the consumer crashes after the provider successfully sends but before committing the Kafka offset, the same event is reprocessed, but the provider recognizes the 
same idempotency key and doesn't send the notification again. We can also maintain an idempotency record in Redis on our side to avoid duplicate processing."







